 ## Overview
-Neural Volumes with Multi-Resolution Hash Encoding (InstantVNR) is a real-time volume rendering method that represents volumetric data using a neural field decoded via multi-resolution hash encoding [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of high computational cost in neural volume rendering by leveraging GPU tensor cores and a native CUDA neural network framework to achieve interactive frame rates (10–60 fps) with high fidelity (PSNR > 30 dB) and compact memory usage (10–1000x smaller than raw data) [S1, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from other neural field methods by supporting three distinct decoding paths: decoding into a dense 3D texture, sample streaming for host-side inference, and fused in-shader inference, while utilizing macro-cell acceleration for empty-space skipping [S1, unofficial].
+Neural Volumes with Multi-Resolution Hash Encoding (InstantVNR) is a method for interactive volume rendering that compresses volumetric data into a neural representation using multi-resolution hash grids [S1, unofficial]. It solves the high inference cost of traditional neural fields by leveraging GPU tensor cores and a native CUDA neural network framework (TCNN) to achieve 10–60 fps rendering [S1, unofficial]. Unlike [HashGrid Encoding](3866-hashgrid-encoding.md) which focuses on the encoding mechanism itself, InstantVNR provides a complete rendering pipeline including macro-cell acceleration for empty-space skipping and three distinct decoding paths (dense texture, sample streaming, and in-shader inference) to support real-time interaction [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method consists of a neural representation trained on volume data and a CUDA-based renderer that supports iterative ray marching with adaptive sampling [S1, unofficial].
-
-### Inputs and Outputs
-*   **Inputs:** Scene JSON containing camera parameters, volume dimensions, transfer functions, and neural network configuration (architecture, hash grid parameters) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Outputs:** Rendered image (color and alpha), training metrics (loss, PSNR, SSIM), and optionally isosurface meshes [S1, unofficial].
+The method defines a scene function $F_\Theta: \mathbb{R}^d \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^k$ using a neural network parameterized by $\Theta$. The input is a continuous coordinate vector (typically 3D spatial position $\mathbf{x}$, potentially augmented with viewing direction $\mathbf{d}$), and the output is a signal vector (e.g., density $\sigma$ and color $\mathbf{c}$) [S1, unofficial]. The network is trained by minimizing a loss between rendered observations and ground truth images [S1, unofficial].
 
